"Cambridge, MA", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "National Bureau of Economic Research", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "DOI": "10.3386/w35663", "type": "report", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 1]], "date-time": "2026-09-01T00:00:48Z", "timestamp": 1788220848000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["CentaurBench: Benchmarking LLM Capabilities on Augmenting vs. Automating Real-World Work Tasks"], "prefix": "10.3386", "author": [{"given": "Pattaraphon Kenny", "family": "Wongchamcharoen", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Kris", "family": "Gulati", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Min Min", "family": "Fong", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Abhishek", "family": "Nagaraj", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "1960", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8]]}, "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 11]], "date-time": "2026-09-11T00:04:31Z", "timestamp": 1789085071000}, "score": 39.70009, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "http://www.nber.org/papers/w35663.pdf"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.3386/w35663", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8]]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 1]], "date-time": "2026-09-01T16:17:34Z", "timestamp": 1788279454458, "version": "build-2803163510"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/copyright-policy/copyright-basics"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Most LLM benchmarks rank models on their ability to automate work tasks. In practice, however, models are often used to assist other (human or LLM) agents. The question that drives model selection is therefore not only which model produces the best output, but which model most improves the work of another (weaker) agent. We introduce a unified framework that evaluates the capability of models to automate and augment another agent&amp;apos;s performance. Across seven economically grounded real-world tasks, an assistant model writes assistance text for a standardized lower-capacity worker model, which produces the deliverable. In automation mode, the assistant produces the output directly. Outputs are scored through blind pairwise comparisons by an LLM judge panel with task-specific rubrics, replicated across ten runs. Rankings across the two regimes are only modestly correlated, and the automation winner loses augmentation on five of seven tasks. Assistance is not reliably positive. The unaided worker outranks every assisted condition on three tasks, and only one model&amp;apos;s guidance beats no guidance on average. These results suggest that automation ability is an incomplete proxy for assistance quality, motivating benchmarks that evaluate models according to the roles they play in human-AI and multi-agent systems.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Institutional subscribers to the NBER working paper series, and residents of developing countries may download this paper without additional charge at &lt;a href=\"http://www.nber.org/papers/&amp;#119;35663\" TARGET=\"_blank\"&gt;www.nber.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;br&gt;</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7377840", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 1]], "date-time": "2026-09-01T15:27:39Z", "timestamp": 1788276459000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["CentaurBench: Benchmarking LLM Capabilities on Augmenting vs. Automating Real-World Work Tasks"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"given": "Pattaraphon Kenny", "family": "Wongchamcharoen", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Kris", "family": "Gulati", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Min Min", "family": "Fong", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Abhishek", "family": "Nagaraj", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 1]], "date-time": "2026-09-01T15:27:39Z", "timestamp": 1788276459000}, "score": 39.2896, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=7377840"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7377840", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 31]], "date-time": "2026-08-31T18:29:07Z", "timestamp": 1788200947264, "version": "build-2803163510"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "group-title": "SSRN", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Elsevier BV", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1767225600000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.uspto.gov/ip-policy/copyright-policy/copyright-basics"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) increasingly generate functionally correct code, but their ability to optimize the performance of complex, real-world software remains unclear. Existing evaluations primarily use self-contained algorithmic tasks harvested from online coding competitions, leaving the relevance of their results to practical software performance engineering (SPE) uncertain. Our study makes two contributions. First, we introduce PerfOpt, a reusable dataset of 65 real-world Java performance-optimization tasks, each with a human-authored solution, project context, tests, and Java Microbenchmark Harness (JMH) microbenchmarks. In contrast to existing LLM performance benchmarks, our focus in PerfOpt is on challenging, real-world optimization problems originating from software development practice rather than coding competitions. Second, we use PerfOpt as well as a sample drawn from an existing state-of-the-art algorithmic benchmark (EffiBench-X) to assess four commercial and open-weight LLMs under four prompting conditions, comparing plausible performance-focused solutions with the original code and human-authored baselines. We show that LLMs appear substantially stronger on EffiBench-X, generating plausible solutions for over 90\\% of tasks, compared to around 60% of tasks in PerfOpt. More importantly, across all tasks, their median speedups on EffiBench-X are 2.1--2.8x, whereas they remain around 1.0x in PerfOpt. We conclude that existing benchmarks such as EffiBench-X draw an overly optimistic picture of current LLM capabilities for real-world SPE. In a real-world benchmark, existing LLMs struggle to both, identify important performance issues and to propose human-competitive fixes. More benchmarks such as PerfOpt are required as a reusable basis for evaluating future tool-supported SPE.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.2139/ssrn.7381504", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 31]], "date-time": "2026-08-31T17:45:12Z", "timestamp": 1788198312000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Beyond Algorithmic Tasks: Do Benchmarks Overestimate LLM Capability for Real-World Performance Optimization?"], "prefix": "10.2139", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0008-7536-8367", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Lirong", "family": "Yi", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Gregory", "family": "Gay", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2777-528X", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Philipp", "family": "Leitner", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "78", "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 8, 31]], "date-time": "2026-08-31T17:45:12Z", "timestamp": 1788198312000}, "score": 31.17971, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://www.ssrn.com/abstract=7381504"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7381504", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}], "items-per-page": 5, "query": {"start-index": 0, "search-terms": null}}}